import{t as e}from"./links-ClIw6rLF.js";var t={id:`F5`,title:`Measurement and Virtual Instruments`,level:`beginner`,stage:`foundation`,summary:`Accuracy and uncertainty, measurement categories, the multimeter, clamp meter, oscilloscope and specialist instruments, used on realistic simulated scenarios and the Phase 5 waveform engine.`,prerequisites:[`F1`,`F2`],recommended:[`F3`],status:`published`,phase:8,competency:`CU-05`,modules:[{id:`F5.M1`,title:`Measurement basics`,summary:`Why a reading is only as good as its accuracy and resolution, how to choose a safe measurement category, and how to record a result so it means something later.`,lessons:[{id:`F5.M1.L1`,title:`Accuracy, resolution and uncertainty`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Distinguish accuracy, resolution and precision in a measurement.`,`Express a measurement result with an appropriate number of significant figures and an uncertainty.`,`Explain why a "more decimal places" reading is not automatically a better one.`,`State typical multimeter accuracy specifications and use them to bound a result.`],introduction:`Every instrument in this laboratory — and every real one on a bench — gives a number that is close to the truth, not the truth itself. Before touching a single probe, you need the three ideas that decide how much to trust that number: [[accuracy]], [[resolution]] and [[precision]]. Get these right and you will read every later lesson's "0.04 Ω" or "452 µF" as an engineering fact with known limits, not as gospel.`,lecture:`### Three different ideas

- **[[resolution|Resolution]]** is the smallest change the instrument can display — the last digit on the screen. A meter reading "11.71 V" has 10 mV resolution on that range.
- **[[accuracy]]** is how close the reading is to the true value. A cheap meter might show four digits of resolution but only be accurate to ±2 % — three of those digits could be noise.
- **[[precision]]** is how repeatable the reading is. Measure the same thing five times and get 11.70, 11.71, 11.71, 11.70, 11.71 V: that is precise. If the true value is actually 11.60 V, it is precise but not accurate.

A cheap bathroom scale that always reads 2 kg light is precise (repeatable) but not accurate. A high-resolution display on an inaccurate instrument is the electrical-measurement version of the same trap.

### Where the specification comes from

Multimeter manufacturers state accuracy as a percentage of the reading plus a number of counts, e.g. **"± (0.5 % + 2 digits)"** on the DC volts range. For a reading of 11.71 V on a 4-digit (0.01 V resolution) range:

- 0.5 % of 11.71 V = 0.059 V
- 2 digits = 2 × 0.01 V = 0.02 V
- Total uncertainty ≈ ±0.08 V, so the true value lies roughly between 11.63 V and 11.79 V.

Every instrument in this lab (and every real one) carries a specification like this. ESR, LCR, insulation and battery testers all have their own accuracy classes, usually worse (a few percent) than a good bench DMM.

### How many figures to report

Report a result to a precision that matches its uncertainty — not to every digit the display shows. "11.71 ± 0.08 V" is honest. "11.714285 V" (what a calculator might show after a division) implies a precision the instrument never had.`,technical:`**Why "+ digits" matters more at low readings.** The percentage term in "0.5 % + 2 digits" shrinks with the reading, but the digit term does not. On a 200 mV range, 2 digits = 0.2 mV, which can dominate the uncertainty of a small reading even though it looks negligible in isolation. Always check the full specification, not just the headline "%" figure, especially near the bottom of a range.

**True value versus measured value.** A "true value" is a convenient fiction: in practice we only ever have a *reference* measurement made with a better-calibrated instrument, traceable (ultimately) to a national standards laboratory. Calibration is the process of comparing an instrument against such a reference and correcting or documenting its error.`,diagrams:[{id:`test-bench`,caption:`Resolution, accuracy and precision shown on the same reading: display digits, the true value, and the scatter of repeated readings.`}],formulas:[{name:`Accuracy specification`,expr:`uncertainty = (accuracy % × reading) + (digits × resolution)`,where:[[`accuracy %`,`the instrument's stated percentage error`,`%`],[`digits`,`stated digit count`,`count`],[`resolution`,`value of the last displayed digit`,`unit`]]}],examples:[{title:`Bounding a resistance reading`,problem:`A DMM specified "± (1.0 % + 3 digits)" on the 400 Ω range (resolution 0.1 Ω) reads 46.8 Ω. What is the uncertainty, and is a 47 kΩ ±5 % resistor (rated 44.65–49.35 Ω... scaled appropriately) consistent with this reading on the correct range?`,steps:[`1.0 % of 46.8 Ω = 0.47 Ω`,`3 digits = 3 × 0.1 Ω = 0.3 Ω`,`Total uncertainty ≈ ±0.77 Ω, so the reading is 46.8 ± 0.8 Ω`],answer:`46.8 ± 0.8 Ω (45.99–47.57 Ω). This overlaps the resistor's tolerance band, so the reading is consistent with a good component.`,check:`The uncertainty (0.8 Ω) is small compared with the 5 % tolerance band (±2.35 Ω) being checked, so this instrument is fit for the job.`},{title:`Why more decimal places is not more accuracy`,problem:`A cheap meter rated "± (3 % + 5 digits)" and a bench meter rated "± (0.1 % + 2 digits)" both measure the same 12.00 V source. Both show 4 digits. Which result should you trust more, and by how much?`,steps:[`Cheap meter: 3 % of 12 V = 0.36 V, plus 5 × 0.01 V = 0.05 V → ±0.41 V`,`Bench meter: 0.1 % of 12 V = 0.012 V, plus 2 × 0.01 V = 0.02 V → ±0.032 V`],answer:`The bench meter is about 13 times tighter, even though both displayed the same number of digits.`}],applications:`This is why a component's test procedure in the library (lesson F5.M4.L1) gives a *good/bad* reading with margin, not a single magic number: it has to stay valid across ordinary meter accuracy. It is also why, in troubleshooting, a reading "close to" the expected value is treated as healthy — a measurement that disagrees by far more than the combined uncertainty is the one worth acting on.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`"The display shows four decimal places, so it must be accurate to four decimal places."`,correction:`Resolution and accuracy are different specifications. Check the accuracy spec before trusting the last digits.`},{mistake:`Quoting a result with more precision than the instrument supports.`,correction:`Round the result to match the calculated uncertainty.`},{mistake:`Assuming all ranges of one meter share the same accuracy.`,correction:`Accuracy specifications are given per range or per function; check the datasheet for the range in use.`}],demo:{title:`Instrument scenarios in this lab`,text:`Every instrument in the Virtual Instruments Laboratory returns a reading together with an explanation. Open the DMM and compare the displayed value with the explanation of what it means.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the digital multimeter`,`dmm`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Digital multimeter`,`dmm`),e.lib(`Resistor`,`resistor`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A meter reads the same value five times in a row, but the value is wrong. This is...`,options:[`precise but not accurate`,`accurate but not precise`,`high resolution`,`uncalibrated resolution`],answer:0,explain:`Repeatable but wrong is precise, not accurate.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A meter rated "± (2 % + 1 digit)" on a range with 0.01 V resolution reads 5.00 V. Uncertainty (V)?`,answer:.11,tol:.05,unit:`V`,explain:`2% of 5.00 = 0.10 V, plus 1 digit = 0.01 V → 0.11 V.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Resolution is...`,options:[`the smallest change the display can show`,`the percentage error of the instrument`,`how repeatable readings are`,`the calibration date`],answer:0,explain:`Resolution is a display property, distinct from accuracy.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why might the "+ digits" term matter more at the bottom of a range?`,options:[`It is a fixed value, so it becomes a larger fraction of a small reading`,`It only applies above half-scale`,`It never matters`,`Digits only apply to AC`],answer:0,explain:`A fixed digit count is proportionally larger for small readings.`}],summary:[`Resolution is display detail; accuracy is closeness to truth; precision is repeatability — they are independent.`,`Manufacturers specify accuracy as "± (% of reading + digits)"; both terms matter, especially near the bottom of a range.`,`Report a result to match its uncertainty, not to every digit the display shows.`,`A reading within the combined uncertainty of an expected value is treated as healthy; a reading far outside it is the one to investigate.`],homework:{id:`F5.M1.L1.hw`,title:`Uncertainty practice`,brief:`Apply "± (% + digits)" to three readings.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A meter "± (0.8 % + 2 digits)", 0.01 Ω resolution, reads 4.00 Ω. Uncertainty (Ω)?`,answer:.052,tol:.05,unit:`Ω`,explain:`0.8% of 4.00 = 0.032, plus 2×0.01 = 0.02 → 0.052 Ω.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Same meter reads 400 Ω on a range with 0.1 Ω resolution. Uncertainty (Ω)?`,answer:3.4,tol:.05,unit:`Ω`,explain:`0.8% of 400 = 3.2, plus 2×0.1 = 0.2 → 3.4 Ω.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A clamp meter "± (2.5 % + 5 digits)", 0.01 A resolution, reads 6.52 A. Uncertainty (A)?`,answer:.213,tol:.05,unit:`A`,explain:`2.5% of 6.52 = 0.163, plus 5×0.01 = 0.05 → 0.213 A.`}],written:[{prompt:`A student says "my new meter has a 6-digit display so it is more accurate than my old 4-digit meter." Explain what is wrong with this reasoning and what the student should check instead.`,model:`Digit count is resolution, not accuracy. Two meters with different digit counts can have the same or even reversed accuracy classes. The student should compare the "± (% + digits)" accuracy specification for the relevant function and range on each meter's datasheet, not the number of displayed digits.`}]}}},{id:`F5.M1.L2`,title:`Measurement categories and probe safety`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain CAT I–IV measurement categories and why they matter.`,`Select a correctly rated meter and probes for a given measurement location.`,`Describe the fuse protection a safe multimeter carries on its current ranges.`,`Identify unsafe probe or lead conditions before use.`],introduction:`A multimeter is not just a sensor; it is a device you hold in your hands while it is connected to a possible fault current. The [[cat-rating|CAT rating]] system exists because a transient (a brief, very high voltage spike) can occur anywhere on a power system, and it is far larger closer to the source. Choosing the wrong instrument for the location can be as dangerous as a wiring fault.`,lecture:`### CAT I–IV

| Category | Typical location | Example |
|---|---|---|
| CAT I | Protected electronic circuits | Signal and low-energy circuits inside equipment |
| CAT II | Single-phase, plug-connected loads | Appliance sockets, extension leads |
| CAT III | Fixed installation, distribution level | Distribution boards, fixed wiring, inverter AC output terminals |
| CAT IV | The origin of the installation | Service entrance, utility meter, outdoor overhead lines |

A higher category number means the instrument (and its probes) is rated to withstand a larger transient without the insulation failing or an internal arc forming. **A CAT II meter used on a CAT III distribution board is unsafe**, even if the steady-state voltage reading would be identical to a CAT III meter — the risk is from a transient, not the everyday voltage.

### Why it is the whole chain, not just the meter

The meter, the leads, and the probe tips must all be rated for the category and voltage in use. A CAT III meter with worn, cracked or CAT II-only replacement leads is no longer a CAT III measurement system. Before use, check:

- Insulation is unbroken, with no exposed metal beyond the tip.
- The exposed metal tip length is short, especially for CAT III/IV work (shrouded probes).
- The fuse is intact and correctly rated (next section).

### Fuse protection on current ranges

A multimeter's current ranges connect almost directly across whatever you are measuring (through the shunt resistor) — this is *why* connecting a current-mode meter across a voltage source is dangerous (F5.M2.L1). A correctly designed meter protects its current ranges with a **high-energy fuse** rated to interrupt a high-current, high-voltage fault safely, often combined with a voltage-dependent resistor or ceramic fuse body to avoid an internal arc. Never bridge a blown fuse with foil or a replacement of the wrong rating "to keep working" — the fuse is there for your protection, not the meter's.

### Working out which category you need

Ask: *what is downstream of me, and how big a fault could occur here?* A measurement at an inverter's 48 V battery terminals is low energy electrically but can still deliver enormous short-circuit current (F1 lesson on battery short circuits) — category ratings are about transient withstand, not just "mains vs DC", and a bench supply measurement is typically CAT I/II while a distribution-board or inverter AC-output measurement is CAT III.`,technical:`**What actually fails.** A transient that exceeds a probe or meter's rating can cause the insulation to break down internally, creating an arc path. The danger is not only electric shock to the user but also an arc flash: an explosive release of energy that can cause severe burns even without direct body contact. CAT ratings are set from statistical studies of transient energy expected at each class of location.

**Digital vs analogue effect on category needs.** The category requirement is about the circuit being measured, not the display technology. A category rating is unrelated to whether the meter is analogue or digital, true-RMS or average-sensing (F5.M3.L2 covers true-RMS).`,diagrams:[{id:`risk-hierarchy`,caption:`CAT I–IV locations along a power system, from protected electronics to the service origin.`}],formulas:[{name:`Fuse margin (reused from F1)`,expr:`I_n ≥ 1.25 × I_b, I_n ≤ I_z`,where:[[`I_n`,`the fuse's rated current`,`A`],[`I_b`,`the design current it must carry`,`A`],[`I_z`,`the cable's current-carrying capacity`,`A`]],note:`The same margin principle from F1 applies to a multimeter's own internal fuse, sized to protect the meter without nuisance-blowing on ordinary use.`}],examples:[{title:`Choosing a category for an inverter job`,problem:`You need to measure the AC output terminals of an installed inverter feeding a distribution board, and separately the 3.3 V logic rail inside its control board. What category does each need at minimum?`,steps:[`AC output terminals, connected to fixed wiring/distribution: CAT III`,`Internal 3.3 V logic rail, a protected low-energy electronic circuit: CAT I is sufficient, though a CAT III meter used throughout is also safe and simpler to manage`],answer:`CAT III for the output terminals (minimum); CAT I is sufficient for the internal logic rail, though using one CAT III-rated meter for both jobs is the simpler and safer practice.`},{title:`Why a correct CAT III meter with the wrong leads is unsafe`,problem:`A technician has a correctly CAT III-rated meter, but its original leads are damaged and have been replaced with unrated leads from a hobby electronics kit. Is the technician still protected to CAT III at a distribution board?`,steps:[`CAT rating applies to the WHOLE measurement chain: meter, leads and probe tips together`,`Unrated hobby leads have no stated transient withstand rating`,`The weakest link in the chain sets the effective protection, not the meter alone`],answer:`No — the measurement system is no longer CAT III rated, regardless of the meter's own rating, because the leads are the weak link.`}],applications:`Every scenario used elsewhere in this course (F5.M2 onward) states where the measurement is taken, because that is exactly the information you need to choose a CAT-rated instrument in real life: the DC bus, the mains socket circuit, and the solar string all imply different categories.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`"A higher voltage range setting is the same as a higher CAT rating."`,correction:`CAT rating is about transient withstand at a given location, not the number on the range dial.`},{mistake:`Using a lower-rated meter "because the reading will be the same".`,correction:`The steady reading may be identical; the risk is from a transient that a lower-rated meter cannot safely withstand.`},{mistake:`Bridging a blown fuse to keep measuring.`,correction:`The fuse protects you from a fault current through the meter. Replace it with the correct type and rating, never bridge it.`}],demo:{title:`Consequences of a wrong connection`,text:`The multimeter in this lab shows exactly what happens when the probes are left in the wrong socket on a live source — the same principle behind fuse protection and category ratings.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the digital multimeter`,`dmm`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Digital multimeter`,`dmm`),e.lib(`Fuse`,`fuse`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A distribution board measurement needs at least...`,options:[`CAT III`,`CAT I`,`Any category`,`CAT II only`],answer:0,explain:`Distribution level is CAT III.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`CAT ratings mainly protect against...`,options:[`transient over-voltage withstand failure`,`everyday voltage reading error`,`display resolution loss`,`battery drain`],answer:0,explain:`The rating is about transient energy withstand.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A CAT III meter with cracked CAT II-only replacement leads is...`,options:[`no longer a CAT III measurement system`,`still fully CAT III`,`safe if used carefully`,`fine for DC only`],answer:0,explain:`The whole chain — meter, leads, probes — must meet the rating.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What should you never do to a blown current-range fuse?`,options:[`Bridge it to keep measuring`,`Replace it with the correct rating`,`Check the rating printed on it`,`Buy a spare of the correct type`],answer:0,explain:`Bridging removes your protection entirely.`}],summary:[`CAT I–IV rates an instrument's ability to withstand a transient at a given point in a power system, from protected electronics (I) to the service origin (IV).`,`The whole measurement chain — meter, leads and probe tips — must meet the category, not just the meter.`,`Current ranges are protected by a high-energy fuse; never bridge it.`,`Choose a category from where the fault energy could come from, not from the steady-state voltage you expect to read.`],homework:{id:`F5.M1.L2.hw`,title:`Category selection`,brief:`Match locations to categories.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A utility service entrance is...`,options:[`CAT IV`,`CAT I`,`CAT II`,`CAT III`],answer:0,explain:`The origin of the installation is CAT IV.`},{id:`h2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A plug-in extension lead supplying a laptop charger is...`,options:[`CAT II`,`CAT IV`,`CAT I`,`CAT III`],answer:0,explain:`A plug-connected load is CAT II.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain why a meter rated CAT III 600 V is not automatically safe for a CAT IV 300 V location, even though 300 V is lower than 600 V.`,model:`Category and voltage are two separate ratings. CAT IV locations can produce much larger transients than CAT III locations even at a lower nominal voltage, because they are closer to the energy source. A CAT III meter, however high its voltage number, is not rated to withstand a CAT IV transient.`}]}}},{id:`F5.M1.L3`,title:`Recording results`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Record a measurement result with instrument, setting, connection and reading together.`,`Explain why a reading without its context is of little use later.`,`Use a consistent format for logging test results across a troubleshooting session.`],introduction:`A number on its own is nearly worthless a week later. "11.71 V" means nothing without knowing where it was measured, with what, on what setting, and under what conditions. This short lesson sets the habit used throughout the rest of the course and the component library: **instrument, setting, connection, reading, and the verdict it supports.**`,lecture:`### The five fields worth recording

1. **What** was measured (the test point or component).
2. **Instrument and setting** (DMM, DC V, autorange; or LCR meter, C mode, 1 kHz).
3. **Connection** (which terminals, in which direction).
4. **Reading**, with its units and, where it matters, its uncertainty.
5. **Conclusion** drawn from the reading (healthy / suspect / faulty, and why).

This is exactly the structure every test procedure in the Component Library (F4, and reused by the component tester in this lab) already follows: instruction, instrument, setting, connection, and a set of labelled readings with their meaning. You are learning the professional habit, not inventing a new one.

### Why "just the number" fails

Six months later, "0.04" means nothing on its own: 0.04 Ω? 0.04 V? Measured where? With what meter, on what range? Was the circuit powered or isolated? A terse log entry like *"Cap bank +/−, ESR meter, ESR mode: 0.04 Ω — healthy (ref. 0.04 Ω good / 1.6 Ω aged)"* answers all of that in one line and lets anyone, including you later, trust or challenge the conclusion.

### Conditions that change the answer

Some readings are meaningless without stating the conditions under which they were taken:

- Load current at the moment of the reading (a battery's loaded voltage depends on it).
- Temperature, where it affects the result (thermal measurements, battery internal resistance).
- Whether the circuit was live or isolated (resistance/continuity/diode tests require isolation).
- Test frequency, for LCR/impedance readings.

Leaving any of these out turns a precise-looking number into a guess.`,technical:`**Traceability.** In regulated industries, a recorded test result is often required to be *traceable*: to a calibrated instrument (with a calibration certificate and date), a documented procedure, and the person who performed it. This course does not require formal calibration records, but the same five fields are the foundation of that discipline.

**Why the component tester in this lab enforces the structure.** The component tester instrument (F5.M4.L2, and reused by F4's test procedures) does not let you skip straight to a bare reading: it always shows the instrument, setting and connection for the current step before you choose a result, and it never gives a verdict without a reading for every step. This is the five-field habit built into the tool itself, so that even a student in a hurry cannot produce a conclusion that is not traceable back to what was actually measured.`,diagrams:[{id:`test-bench`,caption:`The five fields of a complete log entry: instrument, setting, connection, reading and conclusion.`}],formulas:[{name:`Loaded voltage (why load current must be recorded)`,expr:`V_loaded = V_OCV − I · R_int`,where:[[`V_OCV`,`open-circuit voltage`,`V`],[`I`,`load current at the time of the reading`,`A`],[`R_int`,`internal resistance`,`Ω`]],note:`A "loaded voltage" reading is meaningless without the load current that produced it (F5.M4, battery tester).`}],examples:[{title:`A complete log entry`,problem:`Write a log entry for an ESR measurement of a 470 µF bank, found healthy.`,steps:[`What: DC-bus reservoir capacitor bank, +/− terminals`,`Instrument/setting: ESR meter, ESR mode`,`Connection: + to −, circuit isolated and confirmed discharged`,`Reading: 0.04 Ω`,`Conclusion: within the healthy range (ref. 0.04 Ω good / 1.6 Ω aged) — bank is healthy`],answer:`"Cap bank +/−, ESR meter, ESR mode, isolated & discharged confirmed: 0.04 Ω — healthy (ref. 0.04 Ω / 1.6 Ω aged)."`},{title:`Spotting an incomplete record`,problem:`A log simply reads "48 V bus: 47.6 V". What is missing, and why does it matter?`,steps:[`No instrument, setting or connection is stated`,`No conclusion is drawn`,`Without the load current at the time, a small sag from 48 V nominal cannot be judged as healthy or not`],answer:`The entry is missing instrument/setting, connection, load current and a conclusion — as written, a reader cannot tell whether 47.6 V represents a healthy, lightly-loaded reading or an early warning sign.`}],applications:`This exact habit is what the component tester instrument in this lab automates for you: it already prompts for instrument, setting, connection and reading at each step, and produces the conclusion. Troubleshooting write-ups later in the programme (P4/S4) will expect this structure without being told.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Recording only the number.`,correction:`Record instrument, setting, connection and conclusion alongside it.`},{mistake:`Omitting load current when recording a loaded-voltage reading.`,correction:`A loaded voltage without its load current cannot be checked or reproduced later.`},{mistake:`Assuming you will remember the context later.`,correction:`Write it down at the time; memory of "which point" fades within days.`}],demo:{title:`See the structure already in use`,text:`Open the component tester: each step already shows instrument, setting and connection before you choose a reading, exactly the structure this lesson describes.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the component tester`,`component-tester`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Component tester`,`component-tester`),e.proc(`Capacitor ESR test procedure`,`capacitor`,`cap-esr`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which is NOT one of the five fields worth recording?`,options:[`The meter's purchase price`,`Instrument and setting`,`Connection`,`Conclusion`],answer:0,explain:`Price is irrelevant to the measurement's meaning.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why record whether a circuit was live or isolated when logging a resistance reading?`,options:[`Resistance/continuity readings on a live circuit are meaningless or unsafe`,`It changes the units`,`It is required by law everywhere`,`It does not matter`],answer:0,explain:`F5.M2 covers why resistance ranges require isolation.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A battery's "loaded voltage" reading is meaningless without also recording...`,options:[`the load current at the time`,`the meter's serial number`,`the ambient light level`,`the technician's name`],answer:0,explain:`Loaded voltage depends on the current drawn.`}],summary:[`Record instrument, setting, connection, reading and conclusion together — never the number alone.`,`State the conditions (load current, temperature, live/isolated, test frequency) that change what the number means.`,`This is the same structure already used by every test procedure in the component library and this lab's component tester.`,`A number recorded alone cannot be checked, trusted or acted on correctly later.`],homework:{id:`F5.M1.L3.hw`,title:`Write two log entries`,brief:`Practise the five-field format.`,tasks:[],written:[{prompt:`Write a complete log entry for a DMM diode test on a rectifier diode found good (0.55 V forward, OL reverse).`,model:`"Rectifier diode D1, DMM, diode-test mode. Forward (red-anode, black-cathode): 0.55 V. Reverse: OL. Conclusion: healthy junction — conducts forward, blocks reverse."`},{prompt:`Explain why "battery loaded voltage: 11.44 V" is an incomplete record, and rewrite it as a complete one using a load current of 2.86 A.`,model:`It is incomplete because loaded voltage depends on the current drawn — the same battery would show a different "loaded voltage" under a different load. Complete version: "12 V battery, DMM DC V, across terminals, with a 4 Ω load drawing 2.86 A: 11.44 V. Conclusion: sag of about 0.27 V, consistent with a healthy low internal resistance."`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A reading that is repeatable but wrong is described as...`,options:[`precise but not accurate`,`accurate but not precise`,`high resolution`,`uncalibrated`],answer:0,explain:`Repeatability is precision, not accuracy.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A meter "± (1.5 % + 2 digits)", 0.1 V resolution, reads 230.0 V. Uncertainty (V)?`,answer:3.65,tol:.05,unit:`V`,explain:`1.5% of 230 = 3.45, plus 2×0.1 = 0.2 → 3.65 V.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A distribution board requires at minimum...`,options:[`CAT III`,`CAT I`,`CAT II`,`No rating`],answer:0,explain:`Distribution level is CAT III.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What protects a multimeter's current ranges against a fault?`,options:[`A high-energy fuse`,`The display resolution`,`The CAT rating alone`,`Nothing; current ranges are inherently safe`],answer:0,explain:`A correctly rated fuse protects current ranges.`},{id:`k5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which of these belongs in a complete measurement log entry?`,options:[`Instrument, setting, connection, reading and conclusion`,`Only the numeric reading`,`Only the conclusion`,`Only the date`],answer:0,explain:`All five fields give the reading its meaning.`},{id:`k6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`CAT ratings mainly guard against...`,options:[`transient over-voltage withstand failure`,`display rounding error`,`battery drain in the meter`,`resolution loss at low readings`],answer:0,explain:`Category ratings are about transient energy withstand.`}]},practicals:[]},{id:`F5.M2`,title:`Multimeter and clamp meter`,summary:`Operating the digital multimeter across every function and port, the diode test, and measuring current without breaking the circuit with a clamp meter.`,lessons:[{id:`F5.M2.L1`,title:`Voltage, current, resistance, continuity and diode test`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Select the correct function, range and probe socket for voltage, current, resistance, continuity and diode-test measurements.`,`Explain why resistance and continuity ranges require the circuit to be isolated.`,`Predict the consequence of a voltage measurement attempted with the probes in a current socket.`,`Interpret a diode-test reading to judge a junction as good, open or shorted.`],introduction:`The digital multimeter is the one instrument every technician reaches for first, and it is also the one most often connected wrongly. This lesson works through every function — voltage, current, resistance, continuity, diode test — and, deliberately, through the two classic wrong connections, so you experience the consequence in simulation rather than on a real meter.`,lecture:`### The dial (function) and the sockets (ports) are two separate choices

A multimeter has a **function dial** (what to measure) and **probe sockets** (where the red probe plugs in). A correct measurement needs both set together:

| Function | Correct red-probe socket |
|---|---|
| DC V, AC V, Ω, continuity, diode test | VΩ socket |
| DC A, AC A (small currents) | mA socket |
| DC A, AC A (large currents) | 10 A (or higher) socket |

### Why mixing them is dangerous, not just wrong

The current sockets connect to an internal **shunt resistor** of a few milliohms or less — intentionally, so that measuring current (which must be done *in series*, breaking the circuit) disturbs the circuit as little as possible. If the probes are left in a current socket but you connect **in parallel** across a voltage source (as you would for a voltage reading), the meter becomes a near-dead-short across that source. The current that flows is limited only by the source and the shunt — easily tens or hundreds of amperes on a mains or inverter-bus measurement. The meter's internal fuse is there for exactly this event, and should open before anything worse happens; an unfused meter, or a bridged fuse, has no such protection.

The opposite mistake — current function selected, but probes left in the VΩ sockets, connected in parallel as if for voltage — is NOT dangerous but is wrong: the meter's voltage-measuring input has a very high impedance, so almost no current flows through it, and the display reads close to zero. It teaches you nothing about the real current.

### Resistance, continuity and diode test: isolate first

These functions work by the meter applying its OWN small test voltage and measuring the resulting current. If the circuit already has power applied, that outside voltage fights the meter's test source, producing an unstable or simply wrong reading, and can feed current back into the meter's measurement circuit in ways it was never designed for. **Always isolate and prove dead (F1.M3) before switching to Ω, continuity or diode test.**

### Diode test

Diode test applies a small forward current and reads the forward voltage drop directly (unlike a resistance range, because a diode does not obey Ohm's law). A healthy silicon junction reads **about 0.5–0.8 V forward**, and **OL (no reading) reverse**, because it blocks reverse current. A shorted junction reads near 0 V both ways; an open junction reads OL both ways; a leaky junction conducts somewhat in both directions.`,technical:`**Autoranging vs manual ranging.** Most modern DMMs autorange: they pick the best range automatically. On a manual-range meter, starting on the highest range and working down avoids over-range ("OL") on an unexpectedly large signal, and avoids excess resolution loss on a small one.

**Burden voltage.** Even correctly connected, a current measurement inserts the meter's own small resistance (the shunt) into the circuit, causing a small "burden voltage" drop. For most circuits this is negligible; for very low-voltage, low-impedance circuits it can measurably affect the reading — a limitation worth knowing even though this course does not require you to correct for it.`,diagrams:[{id:`test-bench`,caption:`Multimeter function and socket combinations, and the two outcomes of mismatching them: safe-but-wrong (current function, voltage sockets) versus dangerous (voltage function, current sockets, live source).`}],formulas:[{name:`Ohm's law (resistance range)`,expr:`R = V_test / I_measured`,where:[[`V_test`,`the meter's own small test voltage`,`V`],[`I_measured`,`current the test voltage drives through the unknown resistance`,`A`]],note:`Resistance, continuity and diode-test ranges work by applying this known small test source and measuring the result — which an external live supply would disturb.`}],examples:[{title:`Predicting the dangerous mistake`,problem:`A 48 V inverter DC bus is live and delivering 11.6 A. A technician leaves the red probe in the 10 A socket from a previous current measurement, then turns the dial to DC V and connects across the bus. What happens?`,steps:[`The dial says "DC V", but the probe is still physically in the 10 A socket — a near-zero-resistance shunt`,`Connected across 48 V, this is effectively a short circuit through the meter`,`The meter's internal fuse should open to protect it; the 48 V source itself briefly sees a large current spike`],answer:`The meter reads "FUSE" or similarly fails, and the fuse opens — a safe outcome only because the fuse did its job. Always return the red probe to the VΩ socket before switching to a voltage function.`},{title:`Diode test interpretation`,problem:`A diode reads 0.00 V forward AND 0.00 V reverse on diode test. What is its condition?`,steps:[`A healthy junction would read ~0.5–0.8 V forward and OL reverse`,`0 V both ways means no junction barrier at all`],answer:`Shorted junction — replace the diode.`}],applications:`This exact scenario — probes left in the current socket, dial switched to voltage, connected to a live 48 V inverter bus — is reproduced in this lab's multimeter instrument so you can see the "FUSE" result safely before it ever happens on a real bench.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Measuring current with the probes left in the VΩ sockets.`,correction:`Safe but wrong: reads near zero because of the meter's high voltage-input impedance. Move the probes to the matched current socket and break the circuit to insert the meter in series.`},{mistake:`Measuring voltage or resistance with the probes left in a current socket.`,correction:`Dangerous: the current socket is a near-short. Always return to the VΩ socket before a voltage, resistance, continuity or diode-test measurement.`},{mistake:`Measuring resistance on a live circuit.`,correction:`Isolate and prove dead first; resistance ranges assume no external voltage is present.`}],demo:{title:`Operate the multimeter`,text:`Choose a scenario, a function, a port and a test point, then measure. Try the wrong-port combinations deliberately and read the explanation.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the digital multimeter`,`dmm`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Digital multimeter`,`dmm`,`inverter-dc-bus`),e.proc(`Diode test procedure`,`diode`,`diode-test`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Current function, probes in the VΩ sockets, connected in parallel: the meter reads...`,options:[`close to zero (wrong, but not dangerous)`,`the correct current`,`OL always`,`FUSE always`],answer:0,explain:`High input impedance in the voltage sockets means almost no current flows.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Voltage function, probes left in a current socket, connected across a live 48 V source: the result is...`,options:[`effectively a short circuit through the meter — dangerous`,`a normal, safe voltage reading`,`always OL`,`identical to the correct connection`],answer:0,explain:`The current socket is a near-zero-resistance shunt.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why must resistance, continuity and diode-test functions be used on an isolated circuit?`,options:[`They apply their own test voltage, which an external live supply disturbs`,`They need mains power to work`,`They are only accurate when live`,`There is no reason; it is just convention`],answer:0,explain:`The meter's own test source conflicts with an external supply.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A healthy silicon diode on diode test typically reads approximately...`,options:[`0.5–0.8 V forward`,`0 V forward`,`2–3 V forward`,`the full supply voltage forward`],answer:0,explain:`A healthy silicon junction forward-conducts at roughly 0.5–0.8 V.`}],summary:[`The function dial and the probe socket are two separate, both-required choices.`,`Current function with probes in the voltage sockets is safe but wrong (near-zero reading). Voltage/resistance function with probes in a current socket, live, is dangerous (near-short).`,`Resistance, continuity and diode test require an isolated circuit.`,`Diode test reads forward voltage directly: ~0.5–0.8 V good, OL open, 0 V shorted, conducts both ways if leaky.`],homework:{id:`F5.M2.L1.hw`,title:`Multimeter function and port practice`,brief:`Decide the correct function and port for each situation.`,tasks:[],written:[{prompt:`A technician wants to measure the current drawn by a 230 V kettle without cutting the supply cable. What is wrong with this plan, and what should they do instead?`,model:`A multimeter current range must be broken INTO the circuit in series, which does require interrupting the conductor (or using a clamp meter instead, which senses current without breaking the circuit — F5.M2.L2). Cutting a live mains cable to insert the meter is unsafe and the wrong tool for the job; a clamp meter around one conductor is the correct choice when the circuit cannot or should not be broken.`},{prompt:`Explain, step by step, why leaving the probes in the 10 A socket while measuring voltage on a live 230 V circuit is dangerous.`,model:`The 10 A socket connects to a near-zero-resistance internal shunt. Connecting this in parallel across 230 V (as voltage measurement technique requires) presents almost no resistance to the source, so a very large current tries to flow through the meter — effectively a short circuit. The meter's fuse should open to stop this, but the event itself (a sudden short on a live 230 V circuit) is hazardous regardless.`}]}}},{id:`F5.M2.L2`,title:`Clamp meters and DC/AC current`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a clamp meter senses current without breaking the circuit.`,`State the single-conductor rule and explain why clamping both conductors cancels the reading.`,`Describe inrush current and why it affects circuit-protection choices.`,`Choose between a multimeter (in series) and a clamp meter for a given job.`],introduction:`Breaking a live circuit to insert a meter in series is sometimes impractical or unsafe — imagine doing it to every conductor in a distribution board. A **clamp meter** solves this by sensing the magnetic field a current produces around its conductor, with jaws that simply close around the outside of an insulated cable. No circuit interruption, no exposed live connection: just one conductor, clamped alone.`,lecture:`### How it senses current

Any current-carrying conductor produces a magnetic field around it (this is the same physics behind the transformer, F3.M4). A clamp meter's jaws form a magnetic core around the conductor; the field induces a signal proportional to the current, which the meter scales and displays. Because the measurement is magnetic, not electrical contact, the clamp meter never needs to touch a live conductor's copper — only its insulation.

### The one-conductor rule

Clamp **only one current-carrying conductor** at a time. If you clamp both the live and the return (neutral) conductor of the same cable together, their currents are equal and opposite (what goes out must come back), so their magnetic fields cancel almost exactly. The meter reads close to zero — not because there is no current, but because the two fields cancel inside the jaws. This is the most common clamp-meter mistake and gives a dangerously misleading "no current" reading on a cable that may be fully loaded.

### Jaw closure

The jaws must close fully around the conductor to form a complete magnetic loop. Partially closed jaws, or jaws around a conductor bundle with a gap, under-read or give an unstable reading.

### Inrush current

Motors, transformers and some switching power supplies draw a brief but very large **inrush current** at the moment of starting — often several times their steady running current — before settling to normal. A clamp meter is well suited to observing this (many have a "peak" or inrush capture mode), because it does not need to be broken into the circuit beforehand. Inrush current is why motor circuits need breakers or fuses with a deliberate time delay ("slow blow", or motor-rated curves) rather than instant-trip protection, which would nuisance-trip on every start.

### DC-capable clamp meters

Most basic clamp meters sense only AC (using a simple transformer-like core). Measuring DC current (for example, a solar string or a battery feed) needs a clamp meter with a Hall-effect sensor, explicitly rated for DC. Check this before relying on a clamp reading on a DC circuit.`,technical:`**Why cancellation is exact for a balanced pair.** By Ampère's law, the magnetic field measured by the jaws depends on the net current enclosed. Two equal and opposite currents sum to zero net enclosed current, so the field — and the reading — is zero, regardless of how large either individual current actually is. This is the same principle used deliberately, in reverse, by some earth-leakage ("core balance") current transformers, which are designed to read only the *difference* between two conductors' currents.

**Jaw opening and accuracy.** Larger-jaw clamp meters accommodate thicker cables or bus bars but can lose accuracy on small conductors positioned off-centre in the jaw opening; manufacturers specify the accuracy only for a conductor centred in the jaws.`,diagrams:[{id:`test-bench`,caption:`A clamp meter's jaws around a single conductor versus around a live/return pair: the single-conductor field is sensed; the pair's fields cancel.`}],formulas:[{name:`Net enclosed current (Ampère's law, qualitative)`,expr:`I_sensed = I_1 + I_2 + … (signed, by direction)`,where:[[`I_sensed`,`the current the clamp meter reports`,`A`],[`I_1, I_2, …`,`each current-carrying conductor enclosed by the jaws, signed by direction`,`A`]],note:`A live/return pair gives I_1 = −I_2, so I_sensed ≈ 0 even though real current flows.`}],examples:[{title:`Why both-conductors reads zero`,problem:`A clamp meter is placed around both the live and neutral conductors of a cable supplying 6.52 A to a load. What does it read, and why?`,steps:[`The live carries 6.52 A out; the neutral carries 6.52 A back`,`Equal and opposite currents inside the same jaws produce cancelling magnetic fields`],answer:`Close to 0.00 A, even though the cable is carrying real current to a real load — a misleading result from an easy mistake.`},{title:`Sizing protection for a motor`,problem:`A motor draws 3.4 A running but 24.8 A at the instant of starting, decaying to normal within 400 ms. Why would an instant-trip 5 A breaker be the wrong choice?`,steps:[`Running current (3.4 A) is well within a 5 A breaker`,`Starting inrush (24.8 A) is nearly 5 times the breaker rating`,`An instant-trip breaker would open on every start, even though the motor is healthy`],answer:`A motor-rated breaker (or slow-blow fuse) that tolerates a brief surge but still trips on a sustained overload is needed instead.`}],applications:`Clamp meters are the practical way to measure load current on an installed inverter's AC output, a solar string, or a battery feed without disconnecting anything — exactly the scenarios in this lab's clamp-meter instrument.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Clamping both conductors of a cable together.`,correction:`Clamp only one conductor; the fields of a live/return pair cancel and give a false near-zero reading.`},{mistake:`Using an AC-only clamp meter on a DC circuit.`,correction:`A simple transformer-core clamp meter does not sense DC at all; use one explicitly rated for DC (Hall-effect).`},{mistake:`Protecting a motor circuit with an instant-trip breaker sized for running current.`,correction:`Use a motor-rated device that tolerates inrush but still protects against a sustained overload.`}],demo:{title:`See the cancellation yourself`,text:`Clamp a single conductor, then both conductors together, on the inverter output scenario, and compare the readings.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the clamp meter`,`clamp`,`inverter-output`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Clamp meter`,`clamp`),e.lib(`Current sensor`,`current-sensor`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A clamp meter senses current by...`,options:[`the magnetic field around the conductor`,`direct electrical contact with the copper`,`measuring voltage drop across itself`,`counting pulses from a PWM controller`],answer:0,explain:`The jaws form a magnetic sensing loop.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Clamping both the live and neutral of the same cable together gives...`,options:[`a near-zero reading because the fields cancel`,`double the true current`,`the correct current`,`an OL reading`],answer:0,explain:`Equal and opposite currents cancel.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Inrush current is...`,options:[`a brief surge at starting, well above the steady running current`,`the current at full load`,`a fault condition only`,`unrelated to motor starting`],answer:0,explain:`Motors and transformers commonly surge at start.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Most basic (non-Hall-effect) clamp meters can measure...`,options:[`AC current only`,`DC current only`,`both equally well`,`neither`],answer:0,explain:`A simple transformer-core clamp senses only AC.`}],summary:[`A clamp meter senses the magnetic field around a conductor; it never needs to break the circuit.`,`Clamp only ONE current-carrying conductor; clamping a live/return pair together cancels the reading.`,`Inrush current (motors, transformers) is far above steady running current and shapes protection device choice.`,`Most basic clamp meters sense AC only; DC current measurement needs a Hall-effect clamp meter.`],homework:{id:`F5.M2.L2.hw`,title:`Clamp meter scenarios`,brief:`Decide what a clamp meter would read in each case.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A clamp meter around a single live conductor carrying 6.52 A rms reads approximately...`,options:[`6.52 A`,`0 A`,`13.04 A`,`OL`],answer:0,explain:`A correctly clamped single conductor reads the true current.`}],written:[{prompt:`A colleague says a circuit "has no load" because their clamp meter reads 0 A, but the lights it supposedly feeds are clearly on. What is the most likely explanation, and what should they check?`,model:`The most likely explanation is that they clamped both the live and return (neutral) conductors of the same cable together, cancelling the fields and producing a false zero reading. They should check that the jaws enclose only ONE conductor, not the whole cable or cable pair.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Voltage function, probes in a current socket, connected across a live source: this is...`,options:[`dangerous — a near-short through the meter`,`safe and correct`,`only wrong on AC`,`identical to the correct connection`],answer:0,explain:`The current socket is a near-short.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Current function, probes in the VΩ sockets: the meter reads...`,options:[`close to zero`,`the true current`,`FUSE`,`OL`],answer:0,explain:`High input impedance in the voltage sockets blocks current flow.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A healthy diode on diode test reads approximately...`,options:[`0.5–0.8 V forward, OL reverse`,`0 V both directions`,`OL both directions`,`the supply voltage both directions`],answer:0,explain:`A healthy silicon junction conducts forward and blocks reverse.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A clamp meter around both conductors of a loaded cable reads...`,options:[`close to zero, misleadingly`,`the true load current`,`twice the true current`,`OL`],answer:0,explain:`Equal and opposite currents cancel in the jaws.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A basic AC-only clamp meter on a DC solar string reads...`,options:[`nothing useful — it cannot sense DC`,`the correct DC current`,`half the current`,`double the current`],answer:0,explain:`A transformer-core clamp needs a changing field; DC produces none.`}]},practicals:[]},{id:`F5.M3`,title:`Oscilloscope`,summary:`Timebase, volts/division and triggering; reading period, frequency, duty cycle and RMS off a real inverter waveform; and what harmonics and THD look like on screen.`,lessons:[{id:`F5.M3.L1`,title:`Time base, volts per division and trigger`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain what an oscilloscope displays that a multimeter cannot.`,`Set an appropriate time/div and volts/div for a given signal.`,`Explain what a trigger does and why a scope trace is stable without one.`,`Read an inverter output waveform (square, modified-sine, PWM, pure-sine) correctly from the grid.`],introduction:`A multimeter gives you one number. An oscilloscope gives you the whole shape of a signal against time — exactly what you need to tell a square wave from a modified sine wave, or to see switching noise a multimeter would simply average away. This laboratory's oscilloscope reads the same waveform model Phase 5's simulator uses, so what you see here is the identical physics behind every inverter output waveform studied in F3 and built in the Build Your Own Inverter laboratory.`,lecture:`### The screen as a grid

A scope screen is divided into a grid, typically **10 horizontal divisions** (time) by **8 vertical divisions** (voltage). Two settings decide what each division represents:

- **Time/div** — seconds per horizontal division. The whole screen width always shows 10 × time/div.
- **Volts/div** — volts per vertical division. The whole screen height shows 8 × volts/div (±4 divisions from the centre line).

Choosing these well means the waveform fills a useful fraction of the screen: too coarse a time/div compresses several cycles into a blur; too fine shows only a sliver of one cycle. Too coarse a volts/div shrinks the trace to a thin line near the centre; too fine clips the top and bottom off the screen.

### Triggering

Without triggering, the scope would start drawing at a different, essentially random point of the waveform on every pass, and a repeating signal would appear to drift or jitter. A **trigger** tells the scope: "start drawing only when the signal crosses this voltage level, in this direction (rising or falling)." Every subsequent sweep then starts at the same point in the cycle, so a repeating waveform appears stationary on screen.

### Reading an inverter waveform

- **Square wave**: a clean two-level trace, switching instantly between +Vdc and −Vdc.
- **Modified sine**: three levels — +Vdc, 0, −Vdc — held for a fraction of each half-cycle (the conduction angle).
- **PWM / unfiltered SPWM**: a fast train of narrow pulses at the switching (carrier) frequency, whose *average* width traces out a sine shape — only visible properly at a fine time/div.
- **Pure sine (filtered)**: a smooth sinusoid, the LC filter's output once the switching ripple is removed.

Set the time/div so that 2–3 full cycles of the FUNDAMENTAL are visible to judge frequency and shape; switch to a much finer time/div (microseconds, not milliseconds) to see individual PWM pulses.`,technical:`**Why PWM needs two very different timebases.** The fundamental (50/60 Hz) period is tens of milliseconds; the switching (carrier) period at, say, 1 kHz is 1 ms, and at 20 kHz is 50 µs. A single timebase cannot usefully show both the overall sine shape AND individual switching pulses — this is why real oscilloscope work on PWM signals typically alternates between a slow sweep (to see the fundamental envelope) and a fast sweep (to inspect individual pulse edges, rise time and ringing).

**Rising vs falling edge trigger.** Triggering on a rising edge crossing zero captures the waveform starting at the same point every time; triggering on a falling edge captures the mirror-image starting point. Either is valid; consistency between measurements matters more than the choice.`,diagrams:[{id:`test-bench`,caption:`The 10×8 division scope grid, with time/div and volts/div scaling the whole screen, and a trigger level fixing where each sweep starts.`}],formulas:[{name:`Screen span`,expr:`horizontal span = 10 × time/div,  vertical span = 8 × volts/div`,where:[[`time/div`,`seconds per horizontal division`,`s`],[`volts/div`,`volts per vertical division`,`V`]]}],examples:[{title:`Choosing a timebase for 50 Hz`,problem:`You want to see about 2 full cycles of a 50 Hz waveform across the screen. What time/div should you choose?`,steps:[`Period of 50 Hz = 1/50 = 20 ms`,`2 cycles = 40 ms total, across 10 divisions`,`time/div = 40 ms / 10 = 4 ms/div`],answer:`4 ms/div shows about 2 full cycles.`},{title:`Choosing volts/div for a 325 V peak signal`,problem:`An inverter output has a peak of 325 V. What volts/div keeps the trace within the 8-division screen with some margin?`,steps:[`Half the screen height (4 divisions) must cover at least 325 V with margin`,`Try 100 V/div: 4 × 100 = 400 V of headroom above and below centre — comfortable`],answer:`100 V/div (or a similar "nice" setting) keeps a 325 V peak well within the screen.`}],applications:`This is exactly the oscilloscope used throughout the Build Your Own Inverter laboratory's waveform checks and in F3's study of square, modified-sine, PWM and pure-sine outputs — the SAME waveform engine, so a trace captured here matches what you would see probing a real simulated inverter output.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Setting volts/div so fine that the waveform clips off the top/bottom of the screen.`,correction:`Increase volts/div until the full peak-to-peak swing fits within the 8 divisions.`},{mistake:`Trying to see both the fundamental shape and individual PWM pulses on one timebase.`,correction:`Use a slow sweep for the overall shape, then a much faster sweep to inspect individual pulses.`},{mistake:`Assuming an untriggered trace is accurate.`,correction:`Without a trigger, a repeating signal appears to drift; set a trigger level and edge to stabilise it.`}],demo:{title:`Capture a real inverter waveform`,text:`Choose a waveform, set time/div, volts/div and trigger, and watch the trace redraw from this lab's shared waveform engine — the same model behind Phase 5's simulator.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.sim(`PWM and filtering experiment`,`sim-08`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A screen with 10 divisions at 2 ms/div spans how many ms total?`,answer:20,tol:.01,unit:`ms`,explain:`10 × 2 ms = 20 ms.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What does triggering do?`,options:[`Starts each sweep at the same point of the waveform, stabilising the trace`,`Changes the signal's frequency`,`Increases the display resolution`,`Protects the scope from overvoltage`],answer:0,explain:`Triggering fixes the start point of each sweep.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A modified-sine inverter output shows how many distinct voltage levels on screen?`,options:[`Three: +Vdc, 0, −Vdc`,`Two`,`A continuous sine shape`,`One`],answer:0,explain:`Modified sine holds +Vdc and −Vdc for part of each half-cycle, with a zero dwell.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`To inspect individual PWM pulses at a 1 kHz carrier frequency, you should use...`,options:[`a much faster (finer) time/div than for viewing the 50 Hz fundamental`,`the same time/div as for the fundamental`,`a slower time/div`,`volts/div, not time/div`],answer:0,explain:`Individual pulses are far shorter than the fundamental period.`}],summary:[`An oscilloscope shows voltage versus time; time/div and volts/div scale the whole screen.`,`Triggering fixes the start point of each sweep so a repeating waveform appears stationary.`,`Square, modified-sine, PWM and pure-sine inverter outputs each have a distinctive, recognisable trace shape.`,`PWM needs two very different timebases to see both the overall shape and individual pulses.`],homework:{id:`F5.M3.L1.hw`,title:`Timebase and scaling practice`,brief:`Work out scope settings for given signals.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`To see 3 full cycles of a 60 Hz signal, time/div should be about ___ ms/div.`,answer:5,tol:.05,unit:`ms`,explain:`Period = 16.7 ms; 3 cycles = 50 ms / 10 divisions = 5 ms/div.`},{id:`h2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`For a 48 V peak signal, which volts/div setting gives the most comfortable headroom within an 8-division screen?`,options:[`20 V/div`,`2 V/div`,`100 V/div`,`1 V/div`],answer:0,explain:`4 divisions × 20 V/div = 80 V headroom, comfortably above 48 V peak without wasting most of the screen.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain why an untriggered oscilloscope trace of a repeating square wave appears to drift sideways across the screen.`,model:`Without a trigger, each horizontal sweep starts at an arbitrary point in time, not at a fixed point of the waveform's cycle. Because the sweep period and the signal period are not locked together, each sweep begins at a slightly different phase of the signal, so the displayed waveform appears to shift sideways from one sweep to the next.`}]}}},{id:`F5.M3.L2`,title:`Measuring frequency, duty cycle and RMS`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Measure period and frequency directly from a scope trace.`,`Define and read duty cycle from a switched waveform.`,`Explain the difference between true-RMS and average-sensing measurement.`,`Relate a waveform's peak, peak-to-peak and RMS values.`],introduction:`Once a waveform is on screen, the scope's own cursor measurements turn the picture into numbers: period, frequency, peak, peak-to-peak, RMS and duty cycle. This lesson uses exactly those measurements, computed from the same captured trace you saw in the previous lesson.`,lecture:`### Period and frequency from the grid

Count the horizontal divisions spanned by one full cycle and multiply by time/div to get the period T. Frequency is f = 1/T.

### Peak, peak-to-peak and RMS

- **Peak (V_pk)**: the maximum excursion from zero (or from the centre line if DC-offset).
- **Peak-to-peak (V_pp)**: the full excursion from the most positive point to the most negative.
- **RMS (root mean square)**: the value that would deliver the same heating power into a resistor as the AC waveform does — the figure that matters for power calculations (F3.M3).

For a **pure sine**, V_rms = V_pk / √2 ≈ 0.707 × V_pk. For an **ideal square wave**, V_rms = V_pk (it spends all its time at full amplitude). A **modified sine** sits between these, because it holds zero for part of each half-cycle.

### Duty cycle

[[duty-cycle|Duty cycle]] is the fraction of each period a switched signal spends at its higher level: duty = t_on / T, usually expressed as a percentage. A symmetric square wave has 50 % duty cycle. SPWM varies the duty cycle of individual carrier pulses continuously to trace out a sine-shaped average — this varying duty cycle, averaged by the output filter, is what produces the pure-sine output (F3.M5, Build lab project D).

### True-RMS versus average-sensing meters

A cheap multimeter often measures AC by rectifying the signal and reading its average, then multiplying by a fixed factor (1.11) that is only correct for a pure sine wave. On a non-sinusoidal waveform — a square wave, a modified sine, or anything with switching content — this gives the WRONG answer. A **true-RMS** meter computes the actual root-mean-square value regardless of waveform shape and reads correctly on any of them. An oscilloscope's RMS cursor measurement, computed directly from the sampled waveform (as this lab's scope does), is inherently true-RMS.`,technical:`**Why RMS matters for power, not just voltage.** Power delivered to a resistive load is P = V_rms² / R. Using a peak or average value instead of RMS in a power calculation gives a wrong answer by a shape-dependent factor — this is precisely why F3.M3 insists on RMS for power work.

**Crest factor.** The ratio V_pk / V_rms is called the crest factor. A pure sine has a crest factor of √2 ≈ 1.414; a highly peaky waveform (some rectified or distorted signals) has a much higher crest factor, which matters when sizing a component's peak voltage rating even though its RMS current looks modest.`,diagrams:[{id:`spwm`,caption:`Period, peak, peak-to-peak and duty cycle marked on a captured scope trace.`}],formulas:[{name:`Period–frequency`,expr:`f = 1 / T`,where:[[`f`,`frequency`,`Hz`],[`T`,`period`,`s`]]},{name:`RMS of a pure sine`,expr:`V_rms = V_pk / √2 ≈ 0.707 · V_pk`,where:[[`V_pk`,`peak voltage`,`V`]]},{name:`Duty cycle`,expr:`duty = t_on / T`,where:[[`t_on`,`time at the higher level`,`s`],[`T`,`period`,`s`]]}],examples:[{title:`Period and frequency from the grid`,problem:`One full cycle spans exactly 4 horizontal divisions at 5 ms/div. What is the period and frequency?`,steps:[`T = 4 × 5 ms = 20 ms`,`f = 1 / 0.020 = 50 Hz`],answer:`T = 20 ms, f = 50 Hz.`},{title:`RMS of a 325 V peak sine`,problem:`A pure-sine inverter output has a 325 V peak. What is its RMS value, and does this match a "230 V" mains-equivalent output?`,steps:[`V_rms = V_pk / √2`,`V_rms = 325 / √2 = 229.8 V`],answer:`≈ 230 V rms — yes, this is the standard relationship between 230 V rms mains and its ≈325 V peak.`},{title:`Why an average-sensing meter misreads a square wave`,problem:`A 48 V peak ideal square wave is measured by an average-sensing "AC volts" meter that assumes a sine shape (multiplying the rectified average by 1.11) and by a true-RMS meter. Which is correct, and why?`,steps:[`True RMS of an ideal square wave = V_pk = 48 V (it is at full amplitude the whole time)`,`An average-sensing meter applies the sine-only 1.11 factor, which does not apply to a square wave, and reads incorrectly`],answer:`The true-RMS meter reads the correct 48 V; the average-sensing meter reads a different, wrong value because its correction factor assumes a sine shape.`}],applications:`Every power and energy calculation from F3.M3 onward, and every solar/battery load calculation in S2, depends on RMS voltage and current, not peak or average — this is why inverter and power-meter specifications always state RMS figures.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Using an average-sensing ("non-true-RMS") meter on a switched or non-sinusoidal waveform.`,correction:`Use a true-RMS meter, or read RMS directly off an oscilloscope's cursor measurement, computed from the real waveform shape.`},{mistake:`Assuming V_rms = V_pk / √2 for every waveform.`,correction:`That relationship holds only for a pure sine. A square wave has V_rms = V_pk; a modified sine is in between.`},{mistake:`Confusing peak-to-peak with peak.`,correction:`Peak-to-peak is twice the peak for a symmetric waveform about zero; check which the instrument is actually displaying.`}],demo:{title:`Read the cursor measurements`,text:`Capture a waveform on the scope and compare its RMS and duty-cycle readouts against the formulas above for different waveform shapes.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.ckt(`Bridge rectifier exercise`,`ckt-04`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A cycle spans 5 divisions at 2 ms/div. Frequency (Hz)?`,answer:100,tol:.01,unit:`Hz`,explain:`T = 10 ms, f = 1/0.01 = 100 Hz.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`RMS of a pure sine with 170 V peak (V)?`,answer:120.2,tol:.02,unit:`V`,explain:`170/√2 = 120.2 V.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`For an ideal square wave, RMS equals...`,options:[`the peak value`,`peak/√2`,`half the peak`,`always zero`],answer:0,explain:`A square wave is at full amplitude the whole time.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A true-RMS meter differs from an average-sensing meter by...`,options:[`computing the actual RMS regardless of waveform shape`,`always reading higher`,`only working on DC`,`only working above 1 kHz`],answer:0,explain:`Average-sensing meters assume a sine shape; true-RMS does not.`}],summary:[`Period and frequency are read directly from the grid using time/div; f = 1/T.`,`RMS is the power-equivalent value; V_rms = V_pk/√2 only for a pure sine — a square wave has V_rms = V_pk.`,`Duty cycle is the fraction of each period spent at the higher level; SPWM varies it continuously to synthesise a sine average.`,`A true-RMS meter reads correctly on any waveform shape; an average-sensing meter is correct only for a pure sine.`],homework:{id:`F5.M3.L2.hw`,title:`RMS and duty cycle practice`,brief:`Compute RMS and duty cycle for given waveforms.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`RMS of a pure sine with 48 V peak (V)?`,answer:33.9,tol:.03,unit:`V`,explain:`48/√2 = 33.9 V.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A switched waveform is high for 3 ms of a 10 ms period. Duty cycle (%)?`,answer:30,tol:.01,unit:`%`,explain:`3/10 = 30%.`}],written:[{prompt:`A 325 V peak square-wave inverter output and a 325 V peak pure-sine inverter output are compared. Explain why they do NOT deliver the same RMS voltage to a resistive load, even though their peaks are identical.`,model:`RMS depends on the waveform shape, not just the peak. The square wave is at full amplitude (±325 V) for the entire cycle, giving V_rms = 325 V. The pure sine spends most of its time below its peak, giving V_rms = 325/√2 ≈ 229.8 V. Equal peaks do not imply equal RMS, and therefore not equal power delivered to the same resistive load.`}]}}},{id:`F5.M3.L3`,title:`Spectrum and THD`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain what a harmonic spectrum shows that a time-domain trace does not.`,`Relate a waveform's time-domain shape to its harmonic content qualitatively.`,`Define total harmonic distortion (THD) and explain why lower is generally better.`,`Explain why filtering reduces harmonic content.`],introduction:`A time-domain trace (what you have been reading so far) shows voltage against time. A **spectrum** shows the same signal a different way: as a set of pure-sine components at different frequencies — the fundamental plus its harmonics — that, added together, reconstruct the original waveform. This view explains WHY a square wave sounds and behaves differently from a sine wave carrying the same fundamental frequency.`,lecture:`### From time domain to frequency domain

Any periodic, non-sinusoidal waveform can be represented as a sum of a **fundamental** sine wave (at the signal's own frequency) plus **harmonics**: sine waves at integer multiples of the fundamental frequency (2×, 3×, 4× ...), each with its own amplitude. This decomposition is the Fourier series; a spectrum analyser (or a scope's FFT function) displays the amplitude of each of these components as a bar or peak at its frequency.

### What different inverter waveforms look like in the spectrum

- **Pure sine**: essentially one spike, at the fundamental. Very little harmonic content.
- **Square wave**: a strong fundamental plus significant odd harmonics (3rd, 5th, 7th...), which is why an unfiltered square-wave inverter's output is "rougher" on sensitive loads than a sine wave of the same RMS voltage.
- **Modified sine**: fundamental plus odd harmonics too, generally lower in amplitude than a plain square wave because of the zero dwell, but still present.
- **SPWM (unfiltered)**: energy concentrated near the fundamental PLUS large content clustered around the carrier (switching) frequency and its multiples — very different from the low-order harmonics of a square or modified-sine wave. This high-frequency content is exactly what the output LC filter (F3.M2, Build lab project D) is designed to remove, leaving close to a pure sine.

### Total harmonic distortion (THD)

**THD** is a single number summarising how much harmonic content a waveform carries relative to its fundamental:

THD = √(V₂² + V₃² + V₄² + ...) / V₁

where V₁ is the fundamental's RMS amplitude and V₂, V₃... are the RMS amplitudes of the 2nd, 3rd... harmonics. A pure sine has THD ≈ 0. An unfiltered square wave has a high THD (tens of percent). Many sensitive loads (some electronics, audio equipment, certain motors) specify a maximum acceptable THD, which is why pure-sine inverters are preferred for them over cheaper modified-sine designs.

### Why filtering reduces THD

An LC low-pass filter passes the fundamental frequency with little attenuation but strongly attenuates frequencies well above it — exactly where most of a switched waveform's harmonic (and especially switching-frequency) energy lives. This is why a well-designed filtered SPWM inverter can achieve a THD of only a few percent, far below an unfiltered square or modified-sine design.`,technical:`**This course teaches the spectrum qualitatively, not by computing an FFT.** A full Fourier/FFT treatment belongs to a more advanced signal-processing course; here, recognising which waveform shapes carry more or less harmonic energy, and why filtering helps, is the target competency.

**THD is relative to the fundamental, not to a fixed reference.** The same absolute harmonic amplitude gives a lower THD on a waveform with a larger fundamental — this is one reason manufacturers sometimes also quote "THD-F" (relative to fundamental, the usual definition) alongside other conventions; always check which definition a datasheet uses.`,diagrams:[{id:`spwm`,caption:`Time-domain SPWM pulses alongside a qualitative spectrum: energy near the fundamental and clustered around the carrier frequency, both reduced by the output filter.`}],formulas:[{name:`Total harmonic distortion`,expr:`THD = √(V₂² + V₃² + V₄² + …) / V₁`,where:[[`V₁`,`fundamental RMS amplitude`,`V`],[`Vₙ`,`n-th harmonic RMS amplitude`,`V`]]}],examples:[{title:`Estimating THD from two harmonics`,problem:`A waveform has a 230 V rms fundamental, a 30 V rms 3rd harmonic and a 15 V rms 5th harmonic (ignore higher harmonics). Estimate its THD.`,steps:[`THD = √(30² + 15²) / 230`,`= √(900 + 225) / 230 = √1125 / 230 = 33.54 / 230`],answer:`≈ 14.6 % THD.`,check:`A modified-sine-class result: noticeably more distorted than a pure sine (≈0%) but far better than a bare square wave.`},{title:`How filtering changes the spectrum`,problem:`An unfiltered SPWM bridge output has most of its non-fundamental energy clustered around its 1 kHz carrier frequency. After adding an output LC filter with a cutoff well below 1 kHz but above 50 Hz, what happens to the spectrum, qualitatively?`,steps:[`The filter passes frequencies below its cutoff with little attenuation`,`The fundamental (50 Hz) is well below the cutoff and passes through largely unaffected`,`The carrier-frequency content (1 kHz) is well above the cutoff and is strongly attenuated`],answer:`The fundamental remains essentially intact while the carrier-frequency energy is greatly reduced, so the resulting waveform looks much closer to a pure sine — exactly the effect THD improvement from filtering.`}],applications:`This is the direct justification for why F3.M5 and the Build Your Own Inverter project D (pure sine) add an output LC filter after the switching bridge: filtering is how a design moves a high-THD switched waveform toward the low-THD sine that sensitive loads require.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`"Lower frequency content is always the harmonic that matters most."`,correction:`The amplitude of each harmonic matters, not just its order; a smaller high-order harmonic can matter less than a larger low-order one.`},{mistake:`Assuming a modified-sine inverter has the same THD as a square wave.`,correction:`The zero dwell in a modified sine generally reduces (but does not eliminate) harmonic content compared with a plain square wave.`},{mistake:`Thinking filtering removes the fundamental too.`,correction:`A correctly designed low-pass filter is set to pass the fundamental with little attenuation while rejecting higher frequencies.`}],demo:{title:`Compare waveform shapes on the scope`,text:`Capture a square wave, a modified sine and a pure sine at the same fundamental frequency and compare their time-domain shapes as a qualitative guide to their relative harmonic content.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.lib(`PWM controller`,`pwm-controller`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A spectrum display shows a signal as...`,options:[`a fundamental plus harmonic components at different frequencies`,`voltage against time`,`current against voltage`,`temperature against time`],answer:0,explain:`A spectrum is the frequency-domain view.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which waveform has the LOWEST THD?`,options:[`Pure sine`,`Square wave`,`Modified sine`,`Unfiltered PWM`],answer:0,explain:`A pure sine is essentially just its fundamental.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An output LC filter reduces THD mainly by...`,options:[`attenuating frequencies well above the fundamental`,`boosting the fundamental`,`removing the fundamental`,`adding harmonics`],answer:0,explain:`A low-pass filter passes the fundamental and rejects higher frequencies.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`THD with a 100 V fundamental and a single 10 V third harmonic (ignore all others), as a percentage?`,answer:10,tol:.05,unit:`%`,explain:`THD = 10/100 = 10%.`}],summary:[`A spectrum decomposes a periodic waveform into a fundamental plus harmonics at integer multiples of its frequency.`,`Square and modified-sine waveforms carry significant low-order odd harmonics; unfiltered SPWM carries energy near the carrier frequency instead.`,`THD summarises total harmonic content relative to the fundamental; lower THD generally suits sensitive loads better.`,`An output LC filter reduces THD by attenuating frequencies above the fundamental while passing the fundamental itself.`],homework:{id:`F5.M3.L3.hw`,title:`THD estimation`,brief:`Estimate THD from given harmonic amplitudes.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Fundamental 120 V rms, 3rd harmonic 12 V rms only. THD (%)?`,answer:10,tol:.05,unit:`%`,explain:`12/120 = 10%.`}],written:[{prompt:`A customer asks why a cheaper modified-sine inverter is unsuitable for their sensitive audio amplifier, while a pure-sine inverter works fine, even though both deliver the "same 230 V" to the amplifier. Explain the real difference in terms covered in this lesson.`,model:`Both inverters can deliver 230 V RMS, but the modified-sine output carries significant harmonic content (a higher THD) because of its stepped, non-sinusoidal shape, while the pure-sine output is close to a single fundamental frequency with low THD. The audio amplifier is sensitive to the waveform shape, not just the RMS voltage, so the extra harmonic content from the modified-sine inverter can introduce audible distortion or stress that the low-THD pure-sine inverter avoids.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A cycle spans 8 divisions at 1 ms/div. Frequency (Hz)?`,answer:125,tol:.02,unit:`Hz`,explain:`T = 8 ms, f = 1/0.008 = 125 Hz.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`RMS of an ideal square wave equals...`,options:[`its peak value`,`peak / √2`,`half its peak`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`A square wave is at full amplitude for the whole cycle.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A true-RMS meter differs from an average-sensing one because it...`,options:[`computes actual RMS regardless of waveform shape`,`only works on DC`,`always reads lower`,`requires a trigger`],answer:0,explain:`Average-sensing assumes a sine shape.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The waveform with the lowest THD is...`,options:[`pure sine`,`square wave`,`modified sine`,`unfiltered PWM`],answer:0,explain:`A pure sine is essentially just its fundamental.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An output LC filter reduces harmonic content mainly by...`,options:[`attenuating frequencies above the fundamental`,`adding harmonics`,`boosting the carrier frequency`,`removing the fundamental`],answer:0,explain:`A low-pass filter rejects high-frequency content.`}]},practicals:[]},{id:`F5.M4`,title:`Specialist instruments`,summary:`Insulation resistance testing (simulation and safety instruction only), LCR and ESR meters for component condition, and thermal measurement.`,lessons:[{id:`F5.M4.L1`,title:`Insulation resistance testing`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain what an insulation resistance test measures and why.`,`State the safe procedure around an insulation test, including isolation and discharge.`,`Interpret a megohm reading against a guideline minimum.`,`Explain why this course teaches insulation testing in simulation only.`],introduction:`An insulation resistance test checks the health of the insulation separating a conductor from earth (or from another conductor) by applying a test voltage well above normal operating voltage and measuring the tiny leakage current that results. It finds damage — damp, abrasion, rodent damage, ageing — long before it would show up as a working fault. Because the test itself applies a few hundred volts DC, **this course teaches it in simulation only**, with full attention to the safety procedure around it.`,lecture:`### What the test does

An insulation resistance tester ("megger") applies a DC test voltage — commonly 250 V, 500 V or 1000 V, chosen to suit the circuit's normal operating voltage — between a conductor and earth (or between two conductors), and measures the resulting leakage current. Healthy insulation passes almost no current at this voltage, giving a reading in the hundreds of megohms or more. Damaged, damp or aged insulation passes more current, giving a lower reading.

### Reading the result

| Condition | Typical reading (500 V test) |
|---|---|
| New, dry cable | Many hundreds of MΩ |
| Damp conduit run | A few MΩ |
| Physically damaged insulation | Below 1 MΩ |
| Direct contact with earth (fault) | Essentially 0 MΩ |

Installation wiring guidance commonly sets a **guideline minimum of 1 MΩ** for a final circuit tested at 500 V DC — a circuit reading below this should not be energised until the cause is found and corrected. (Always follow the specific regulation applicable in your jurisdiction; this course teaches the general principle, not a specific national standard.)

### The safety procedure

Because the test applies a voltage well above normal operating levels:

1. **Isolate** the circuit and lock off the supply (F1.M3).
2. **Prove dead** with a proving unit, before and after.
3. **Disconnect** anything that cannot withstand the test voltage — sensitive electronics, surge arrestors, some sensors.
4. **Discharge** the circuit (and any capacitance in it) before AND after the test — the test itself leaves a charge on the circuit's own capacitance, which must be discharged before it is touched again.

### Why this course never applies a real test voltage

An insulation tester deliberately applies a hazardous voltage to a circuit that is, by definition, not yet confirmed safe (that is the point of the test). This combination of "intentionally elevated voltage" and "circuit of unknown condition" is exactly the situation this programme's simulation-only policy exists for: you learn the procedure, the readings, and the judgement calls completely safely here, and apply real tests only under qualified supervision with real equipment.`,technical:`**Why DC, not AC, for the test voltage.** A DC test voltage avoids the capacitive charging current that an AC test would also measure, isolating the TRUE leakage (resistive) component of the current. Most insulation testers also include a timer function (the resistance reading tends to rise over the first minute as the circuit's own capacitance finishes charging) for a more stable final reading.

**Polarization index.** For larger installations, insulation resistance is sometimes read at 1 minute and 10 minutes into the test; a rising ratio (polarization index) indicates healthy, dry insulation, while a flat or falling ratio can indicate contamination or moisture, even if the absolute reading looks acceptable.`,diagrams:[{id:`isolation-flow`,caption:`The safe-isolation and discharge sequence around an insulation resistance test, before and after applying the test voltage.`}],formulas:[{name:`Insulation resistance (Ohm's law, at the test voltage)`,expr:`R_insulation = V_test / I_leakage`,where:[[`V_test`,`applied DC test voltage`,`V`],[`I_leakage`,`the small leakage current the test detects`,`A`]],note:`Healthy insulation passes almost no current, giving a very high resistance reading; damaged insulation passes more.`}],examples:[{title:`Judging a borderline reading`,problem:`A circuit tested at 500 V DC reads 4.2 MΩ. Is it acceptable against the 1 MΩ guideline minimum, and would you energise it without further investigation?`,steps:[`4.2 MΩ is above the 1 MΩ guideline minimum`,`However, it is far below a healthy new cable's reading (hundreds of MΩ), suggesting dampness or early degradation`],answer:`Technically acceptable against the bare guideline, but worth investigating the cause (likely dampness) before leaving it in service — a reading this far below a healthy new cable is a warning sign, not a clean pass.`},{title:`A direct earth fault`,problem:`A conductor tested at 500 V DC reads close to 0 MΩ to earth. What does this mean, and is it safe to energise?`,steps:[`A reading near 0 MΩ means almost no resistance separates the conductor from earth`,`This is consistent with a direct contact fault, not degraded insulation`],answer:`No — this is effectively a dead short to earth. The circuit must not be energised until the fault is located and repaired.`}],applications:`Insulation testing is the standard check after a cable is newly installed, after work that may have disturbed insulation, or during periodic inspection of fixed wiring and installed inverter/solar systems — a key part of the commissioning sequence covered in S2's Commissioning module.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Testing a circuit with sensitive electronics still connected.`,correction:`Disconnect anything that cannot withstand the test voltage before testing.`},{mistake:`Touching the circuit immediately after the test without discharging it.`,correction:`The test itself leaves a charge on the circuit's own capacitance; discharge before and after.`},{mistake:`Treating "above the guideline minimum" as "perfectly healthy".`,correction:`A reading far below a healthy new cable's typical value is worth investigating even if it technically passes the minimum.`}],demo:{title:`Walk through the safety procedure`,text:`This instrument requires you to confirm every safety step before it will apply a (simulated) test voltage — exactly the discipline the real procedure demands.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the insulation resistance tester`,`insulation`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Insulation resistance tester`,`insulation`),e.lib(`Protective earth`,`protective-earth`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An insulation resistance test measures...`,options:[`leakage resistance between a conductor and earth (or another conductor) at an elevated test voltage`,`the conductor's DC resistance along its length`,`the supply frequency`,`the conductor's current rating`],answer:0,explain:`It specifically checks insulation health, not conductor resistance.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why must a circuit be discharged AFTER an insulation test, not just before?`,options:[`The test itself leaves a charge on the circuit's own capacitance`,`The tester needs to cool down`,`It resets the tester's calibration`,`It is not necessary after, only before`],answer:0,explain:`The applied test voltage charges the circuit's capacitance.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A common guideline minimum for a final circuit at 500 V DC test is...`,options:[`1 MΩ`,`1 Ω`,`1 kΩ`,`100 Ω`],answer:0,explain:`A widely used general guideline, subject to local regulation.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why is this instrument simulation-only in this course?`,options:[`It intentionally applies a hazardous test voltage to a circuit of unknown condition`,`It is too expensive to model`,`It only works on DC circuits`,`There is no real safety concern, it is just convention`],answer:0,explain:`The combination of elevated voltage and unknown circuit condition is the exact hazard the simulation-only policy addresses.`}],summary:[`An insulation resistance test applies an elevated DC voltage and measures leakage resistance to judge insulation health.`,`Readings in the hundreds of MΩ are healthy; a few MΩ suggests dampness or ageing; below 1 MΩ is commonly a guideline failure.`,`Isolate, prove dead, disconnect sensitive equipment, test, then discharge before AND after.`,`This instrument is simulation and safety instruction only in this course; real testing needs qualified supervision and real equipment.`],homework:{id:`F5.M4.L1.hw`,title:`Insulation test judgement`,brief:`Judge readings and safety steps.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A reading of 0.6 MΩ at 500 V DC against a 1 MΩ guideline minimum is...`,options:[`below the guideline: do not energise until investigated`,`acceptable`,`irrelevant without the frequency`,`only a concern above 1000 V`],answer:0,explain:`0.6 MΩ < 1 MΩ.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain, step by step, the full safety sequence around an insulation resistance test, and why the order of steps matters.`,model:`Isolate the circuit and lock off the supply, then prove dead with a proving unit to confirm no voltage remains. Disconnect anything that cannot withstand the elevated test voltage. Apply the test and read the result. Afterward, discharge the circuit again, because the test itself leaves a charge on the circuit's own capacitance. The order matters because each step depends on the one before it being true: proving dead before testing confirms it is safe to touch to disconnect sensitive items, and discharging after is necessary precisely because the test just charged the circuit.`}]}}},{id:`F5.M4.L2`,title:`LCR and ESR meters`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain what an LCR meter measures and why it needs a test frequency.`,`Explain why ESR, not capacitance alone, is the key health check for an electrolytic capacitor.`,`Relate reactance at a given frequency to the same impedance formulas from F3.M2.`,`Use good/degraded/open/shorted reference values to judge a capacitor or inductor.`],introduction:`A multimeter's ohms range cannot properly characterise a capacitor or inductor — a capacitor passes no DC at all, and an inductor looks like a plain wire to a DC ohmmeter. LCR and ESR meters apply an AC test signal instead, and this lesson's readings are taken directly from the component library's own reference data (F4), so what you see here matches what the library already teaches about capacitor and inductor failure.`,lecture:`### What an LCR meter measures

An **LCR meter** applies a small AC test signal at a chosen frequency and measures the component's **inductance (L)**, **capacitance (C)**, or **impedance (Z)**, depending on the mode selected. Because capacitive and inductive reactance both depend on frequency (F3.M2), the TEST FREQUENCY matters: a healthy part can look quite different at 100 Hz versus 20 kHz, and manufacturers usually specify capacitance/inductance at a stated reference frequency (often 100 Hz or 1 kHz for larger components).

**Z = √(X² + R²)** — the same impedance formula from F3.M2 — where X is the reactance (X_C = 1/2πfC for a capacitor, X_L = 2πfL for an inductor) and R is the component's series resistance (ESR for a capacitor, DC resistance for an inductor).

### ESR: the real health check for an electrolytic capacitor

A capacitor's **equivalent series resistance (ESR)** represents internal losses — in the electrolyte, the foil, the connections. As an electrolytic capacitor ages, its electrolyte dries out: capacitance falls AND ESR rises, often well before the part fails outright. Because ESR shows up directly as heating under ripple current, **a rising ESR is usually the first and most practically important sign of an ageing capacitor** — more diagnostic, in many cases, than capacitance alone. The component library's own reference values make this concrete: a healthy 470 µF capacitor reads about 452 µF / 0.04 Ω ESR; an aged one reads about 210 µF / 1.6 Ω — the ESR has risen by a factor of 40, far more dramatically than the capacitance has fallen.

### Reading good/degraded/open/shorted

| Condition | Capacitor (C / ESR) | Inductor (L / DCR) |
|---|---|---|
| Good | Near nominal / low ESR | Near nominal / low DCR |
| Degraded | Reduced C / high ESR | Reduced L (shorted turns), DCR may look normal |
| Open | OL, no reading | OL, no reading |
| Shorted | Near-zero or erratic C, near-zero ESR | — |

Note the trap in the "shorted" row: a near-zero ESR on its own LOOKS like "very healthy", but combined with no measurable capacitance it actually indicates a short, not a good part. **Always read ESR together with capacitance**, never ESR alone.

### Why an inductor needs both L and DC resistance checked

Shorted turns in an inductor's winding reduce its effective inductance (less magnetic coupling reaching the core) while its DC resistance can still look roughly normal, because only a small fraction of turns may be shorted. This is why the component library's inductor test checks DC resistance AND inductance as separate steps — DC resistance alone would miss a shorted-turns fault.`,technical:`**Test signal amplitude.** LCR meters use a small test signal (often under 1 V), deliberately kept low so as not to forward-bias any semiconductor junctions that might be in parallel with the part under test, and to stay within the component's own small-signal linear region.

**Frequency dependence as a diagnostic tool, not just a specification detail.** Sweeping the test frequency and plotting impedance (as F3.M2 covers conceptually) can reveal a capacitor's self-resonant frequency, above which its own parasitic inductance dominates and it stops behaving as a capacitor at all — a real effect relevant to high-frequency switching design, beyond what this introductory treatment requires you to calculate.`,diagrams:[{id:`rc-curve`,caption:`Capacitor impedance magnitude falling with frequency, and inductor impedance rising with frequency — the same X_C/X_L relationships from F3.M2, now read off an LCR meter.`}],formulas:[{name:`Capacitive reactance`,expr:`X_C = 1 / (2πfC)`,where:[[`X_C`,`capacitive reactance`,`Ω`],[`f`,`test frequency`,`Hz`],[`C`,`capacitance`,`F`]]},{name:`Inductive reactance`,expr:`X_L = 2πfL`,where:[[`X_L`,`inductive reactance`,`Ω`],[`L`,`inductance`,`H`]]},{name:`Impedance magnitude`,expr:`Z = √(X² + R²)`,where:[[`R`,`ESR (capacitor) or DC resistance (inductor)`,`Ω`]]}],examples:[{title:`Spotting a shorted capacitor from ESR alone`,problem:`A capacitor reads ESR = 0.002 Ω (very low) and capacitance = 0 µF. Is this a healthy capacitor?`,steps:[`Low ESR alone would look "good"`,`But zero measurable capacitance means it is not behaving as a capacitor at all`],answer:`No — this combination indicates a shorted capacitor, not a healthy one. Always check ESR together with capacitance.`},{title:`Impedance of a 470 µF capacitor at two frequencies`,problem:`Using the healthy reference values (C = 452 µF, ESR = 0.04 Ω), estimate the impedance magnitude at 100 Hz and at 20 kHz.`,steps:[`X_C(100 Hz) = 1/(2π×100×452e-6) ≈ 3.52 Ω; Z ≈ √(3.52² + 0.04²) ≈ 3.52 Ω`,`X_C(20 kHz) = 1/(2π×20000×452e-6) ≈ 0.0176 Ω; Z ≈ √(0.0176² + 0.04²) ≈ 0.044 Ω`],answer:`Impedance falls from about 3.52 Ω at 100 Hz to about 0.044 Ω at 20 kHz — the capacitor "filters" far more effectively at the higher frequency, which is exactly why it is chosen to bypass high-frequency switching ripple rather than low-frequency mains hum.`}],applications:`This is the same reasoning the component tester instrument automates, and the same reference data used by F4's capacitor and inductor test procedures — one consistent set of numbers across the library, the LCR/ESR instrument, and the component tester.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Judging a capacitor by ESR alone.`,correction:`A very low ESR with no measurable capacitance indicates a short, not health. Check both together.`},{mistake:`Comparing a capacitance reading at one frequency with a spec at another without accounting for frequency dependence.`,correction:`Measure at (or close to) the frequency the component is specified at, where practical.`},{mistake:`Checking only DC resistance on a suspect inductor.`,correction:`Shorted turns can leave DC resistance looking roughly normal; also measure inductance.`}],demo:{title:`Measure a real capacitor/inductor case`,text:`Select a good, degraded, open or shorted case and measure C, L, ESR, DCR or Z — the good-case values match the component library exactly.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the LCR meter`,`lcr`)},activities:[e.instrument(`LCR meter`,`lcr`),e.proc(`Capacitor ESR test procedure`,`capacitor`,`cap-esr`),e.proc(`Inductor test procedure`,`inductor`,`inductor-lcr`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What does ESR represent in an electrolytic capacitor?`,options:[`Internal losses (electrolyte, foil, connections) in series with the ideal capacitance`,`The capacitor's voltage rating`,`Its physical size`,`Its leakage current only`],answer:0,explain:`ESR models the real losses in the device.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A near-zero ESR with no measurable capacitance most likely indicates...`,options:[`a shorted capacitor`,`an excellent, healthy capacitor`,`an open capacitor`,`a correctly rated capacitor`],answer:0,explain:`Always check ESR together with capacitance.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why does capacitive reactance fall as test frequency rises?`,options:[`X_C = 1/(2πfC): frequency is in the denominator`,`Capacitors behave as resistors at high frequency`,`It does not change with frequency`,`It only falls for inductors`],answer:0,explain:`Reactance is inversely proportional to frequency for a capacitor.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A suspect inductor with normal DC resistance but reduced inductance most likely has...`,options:[`shorted turns`,`an open winding`,`the wrong core material`,`no fault`],answer:0,explain:`Shorted turns reduce effective inductance without necessarily changing overall DC resistance much.`}],summary:[`An LCR meter measures L, C or Z at a chosen AC test frequency; reactance depends on that frequency (F3.M2).`,`ESR, not capacitance alone, is usually the first practical sign of an ageing electrolytic capacitor.`,`Always read ESR together with capacitance — near-zero ESR with no capacitance means shorted, not healthy.`,`An inductor needs both DC resistance AND inductance checked, because shorted turns can leave DC resistance looking normal.`],homework:{id:`F5.M4.L2.hw`,title:`LCR/ESR judgement`,brief:`Apply the good/degraded/open/shorted table.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`X_C of a 100 µF capacitor at 1 kHz (Ω)?`,answer:1.59,tol:.03,unit:`Ω`,explain:`X_C = 1/(2π×1000×100e-6) ≈ 1.59 Ω.`}],written:[{prompt:`A technician measures a capacitor's capacitance only, finds it close to the marked value, and declares it healthy. Explain what they may be missing and why.`,model:`Capacitance alone does not catch a rising ESR, which is often the earliest and most practically important sign of an ageing electrolytic capacitor. A capacitor can retain close-to-nominal capacitance while its ESR has risen significantly, causing excess heating under ripple current and eventual failure. The technician should also measure ESR before declaring the capacitor healthy.`}]}}},{id:`F5.M4.L3`,title:`Thermal imaging and temperature probes`,minutes:20,content:{objectives:[`Explain what a thermal (infrared) camera measures, and its limitations.`,`Explain how a contact temperature probe differs from a non-contact infrared reading.`,`Interpret a thermal reading against a device's rated operating temperature.`,`Relate excess heating to likely causes in an inverter.`],introduction:`Heat is one of the clearest symptoms of an electrical problem: a loose connection, an overloaded component or a failing device almost always runs hotter than it should before it fails outright. This lesson covers how thermal measurement works and its limits, building on the heat-sink and cooling content already in the component library.`,lecture:`### Non-contact infrared versus contact probes

A **thermal (infrared) camera** measures the infrared radiation a surface emits and converts it to a temperature map, without touching anything — useful for scanning a whole board or enclosure quickly and spotting a hot spot you were not specifically looking for. A **contact temperature probe** (thermocouple or similar) touches the surface directly and reads a single point, generally more accurate for that one point but requiring you to already know where to check.

### The emissivity trap

Infrared cameras assume a surface **emissivity** (how efficiently it radiates, relative to a perfect "black body") to convert the detected radiation into a temperature. Shiny, reflective surfaces (bare metal, some heat-sink finishes) have low emissivity and can read significantly LOWER than their true temperature on an infrared camera set for the wrong emissivity — a dangerous trap if you trust the number without checking. Matte, dark surfaces are closer to ideal and read more reliably. When in doubt, cross-check a suspicious infrared reading with a contact probe.

### Interpreting a reading

Compare the measured temperature against the device's rated maximum operating (junction or case) temperature, not against "how hot it feels". A heat sink that feels hot to the touch may still be well within a power MOSFET's rated junction temperature; conversely, a device can be dangerously overheating well before it feels alarmingly hot to a brief touch.

### Likely causes of excess heating in an inverter

- A loose or corroded connection (increased resistance, localised heating at the joint, not spread across the conductor).
- An undersized conductor or heat sink for the actual load.
- A failing switching device with increased on-resistance or switching losses.
- Blocked or failed cooling (clogged heat sink fins, a stalled fan).`,technical:`**Thermal resistance and rise above ambient.** A component's temperature rise above ambient is set by its power dissipation and the thermal resistance of its path to ambient (device → heat sink → air), covered conceptually in the component library's cooling entries. The same absolute temperature reading means something different on a 20 °C day versus a 40 °C day — always note ambient temperature alongside a thermal reading, consistent with F5.M1.L3's "record the conditions" principle.

**Thermal imaging cannot see inside an enclosure.** Infrared cameras only see the surface actually in their line of sight; a hot internal component behind a closed metal enclosure will not show up until its heat has conducted to a visible outer surface, by which point the internal temperature may already be considerably higher.`,diagrams:[{id:`thermal-network`,caption:`Heat flow from a switching device through its heat sink to ambient air, and where a thermal camera and a contact probe each "see" the resulting temperature.`}],formulas:[{name:`Temperature rise above ambient`,expr:`ΔT = P_diss × R_thermal`,where:[[`ΔT`,`rise above ambient temperature`,`°C`],[`P_diss`,`power dissipated by the device`,`W`],[`R_thermal`,`total thermal resistance to ambient`,`°C/W`]],note:`Always record ambient temperature alongside a thermal reading — the same absolute reading means something different on a hot day versus a cool one.`}],examples:[{title:`A misleading low reading`,problem:`An infrared camera reads a bare, shiny aluminium heat sink at 38 °C, but a contact probe on the same spot reads 61 °C. What is the likely explanation?`,steps:[`Bare, shiny aluminium has low emissivity`,`An infrared reading set for a higher (incorrect) emissivity under-reads a low-emissivity surface`],answer:`The camera's emissivity setting does not match the shiny surface, causing it to under-read. The contact probe's 61 °C is the more trustworthy figure here; cross-checking caught the error.`},{title:`Estimating temperature rise`,problem:`A device dissipates 8 W through a heat sink with a total thermal resistance to ambient of 4 °C/W, in a 30 °C room. Estimate its case temperature.`,steps:[`ΔT = P_diss × R_thermal = 8 × 4 = 32 °C`,`Case temperature = ambient + ΔT = 30 + 32`],answer:`≈ 62 °C case temperature — compare this against the device's rated maximum, not against how hot it feels.`}],applications:`Thermal checks are a standard part of commissioning and troubleshooting an installed inverter (S2's commissioning module, and the Repair and Troubleshooting Workshop ahead), typically run alongside electrical measurements, not instead of them.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Trusting an infrared reading on a shiny metal surface without checking emissivity.`,correction:`Cross-check with a contact probe, or set the camera's emissivity correctly for the surface.`},{mistake:`Judging "too hot" by feel.`,correction:`Compare the measured temperature against the specific device's rated maximum, not a subjective impression.`},{mistake:`Assuming a cool-looking enclosure means everything inside is fine.`,correction:`Infrared imaging only sees visible surfaces; an internal hot spot may not yet have conducted to the outside.`}],demo:{title:`Review the component library's thermal reference`,text:`The heat sink and cooling fan entries in the component library describe thermal resistance and rated operating ranges relevant to this lesson.`,link:e.lib(`Heat sink`,`heat-sink`)},activities:[e.lib(`Heat sink`,`heat-sink`),e.lib(`Cooling fan`,`cooling-fan`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An infrared camera on a shiny, low-emissivity surface tends to...`,options:[`under-read the true temperature`,`over-read the true temperature`,`read perfectly regardless of surface`,`only work on metal`],answer:0,explain:`Low emissivity causes under-reading unless corrected for.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A contact temperature probe, compared with an infrared camera, is generally...`,options:[`more accurate for the single point it touches, but cannot scan a wide area quickly`,`always less accurate`,`unaffected by surface finish and always faster`,`only usable on DC circuits`],answer:0,explain:`Contact probes trade scanning speed for single-point accuracy.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The correct way to judge "too hot" is to compare against...`,options:[`the specific device's rated maximum operating temperature`,`how hot it feels to a brief touch`,`room temperature only`,`the inverter's weight`],answer:0,explain:`Subjective feel is not a reliable or quantitative check.`}],summary:[`Infrared cameras read surface temperature without contact but depend on a correct emissivity setting; contact probes read one point more reliably.`,`Shiny, low-emissivity surfaces can under-read significantly on an infrared camera; cross-check with a contact probe when in doubt.`,`Judge heating against the specific device's rated maximum, not by feel.`,`Infrared imaging only sees visible surfaces; internal hot spots can exist before they are visible from outside an enclosure.`],homework:{id:`F5.M4.L3.hw`,title:`Thermal interpretation`,brief:`Explain thermal readings.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A device dissipates 5 W with a total thermal resistance to ambient of 6 °C/W, in a 25 °C room. Case temperature (°C)?`,answer:55,tol:.02,unit:`°C`,explain:`ΔT = 5 × 6 = 30 °C; case = 25 + 30 = 55 °C.`}],written:[{prompt:`An infrared scan of an inverter enclosure shows nothing unusual, but the unit is intermittently shutting down on thermal protection. Explain why the infrared result does not rule out a thermal problem.`,model:`An infrared camera only sees the temperature of surfaces within its line of sight. If the enclosure is closed, an internal component (such as a switching device or a connection) can be significantly overheating without that heat yet having conducted to a visible outer surface, especially during a brief scan. The thermal protection shutdown is real evidence of a genuine internal thermal problem that an external infrared scan of the closed enclosure would not detect.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A 500 V DC insulation test guideline minimum for a final circuit is commonly...`,options:[`1 MΩ`,`1 Ω`,`1 kΩ`,`10 Ω`],answer:0,explain:`A widely used general guideline.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A rising ESR in an electrolytic capacitor usually indicates...`,options:[`an ageing capacitor, often before capacitance drops much`,`a healthy capacitor`,`a wrong voltage rating`,`nothing significant`],answer:0,explain:`ESR often rises as the first sign of ageing.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Near-zero ESR with no measurable capacitance indicates...`,options:[`a shorted capacitor`,`an excellent capacitor`,`an open capacitor`,`a correctly rated capacitor`],answer:0,explain:`Check ESR and capacitance together.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A shiny, low-emissivity surface on an infrared camera tends to...`,options:[`under-read the true temperature`,`over-read the true temperature`,`read perfectly`,`disable the camera`],answer:0,explain:`Low emissivity causes under-reading unless corrected.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why is the insulation resistance tester simulation-only in this course?`,options:[`It intentionally applies a hazardous voltage to a circuit of unknown condition`,`It is not a real instrument`,`It only works in theory`,`There is no safety concern`],answer:0,explain:`The combination of elevated voltage and unknown circuit condition is the specific hazard addressed.`}]},practicals:[]}],exam:{pass:.8,minutes:45,attempts:3,cooldownHours:24,questions:[{id:`e1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A reading that is repeatable but wrong is...`,options:[`precise but not accurate`,`accurate but not precise`,`high resolution`,`uncalibrated`],answer:0,explain:`Repeatability is precision, not accuracy.`},{id:`e2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A meter "± (1.0 % + 2 digits)", 0.01 Ω resolution, reads 4.00 Ω. Uncertainty (Ω)?`,answer:.062,tol:.05,unit:`Ω`,explain:`1% of 4.00 = 0.04, plus 2×0.01 = 0.02 → 0.06 Ω.`},{id:`e3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A distribution board measurement needs at minimum...`,options:[`CAT III`,`CAT I`,`CAT II`,`No rating`],answer:0,explain:`Distribution level is CAT III.`},{id:`e4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Voltage function, probes left in a current socket, connected across a live source: this is...`,options:[`dangerous — a near-short through the meter`,`safe and correct`,`only a concern on AC`,`identical to the correct connection`],answer:0,explain:`The current socket is a near-zero-resistance shunt.`},{id:`e5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Current function, probes left in the VΩ sockets: the meter reads...`,options:[`close to zero, safe but wrong`,`the true current`,`FUSE`,`the supply voltage`],answer:0,explain:`High input impedance blocks current flow.`},{id:`e6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A healthy diode on diode test reads approximately...`,options:[`0.5–0.8 V forward, OL reverse`,`0 V both directions`,`OL both directions`,`the supply voltage both directions`],answer:0,explain:`A healthy junction conducts forward and blocks reverse.`},{id:`e7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why must resistance, continuity and diode test be used on an isolated circuit?`,options:[`They apply their own test voltage, disturbed by an external supply`,`They need mains power`,`They are only accurate when live`,`No reason; it is convention`],answer:0,explain:`The meter's own test source conflicts with external power.`},{id:`e8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A clamp meter senses current by...`,options:[`the magnetic field around a conductor`,`direct electrical contact`,`counting PWM pulses`,`measuring self-heating`],answer:0,explain:`The jaws form a magnetic sensing loop.`},{id:`e9`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Clamping both conductors of a loaded cable together gives...`,options:[`a near-zero reading, misleadingly`,`the true current`,`double the true current`,`OL`],answer:0,explain:`Equal and opposite currents cancel.`},{id:`e10`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Inrush current at motor start is typically...`,options:[`several times the steady running current, briefly`,`always less than running current`,`only present in DC motors`,`irrelevant to protection sizing`],answer:0,explain:`This is why motor circuits need delay-rated protection.`},{id:`e11`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A cycle spans 5 divisions at 4 ms/div. Frequency (Hz)?`,answer:50,tol:.02,unit:`Hz`,explain:`T = 20 ms, f = 1/0.02 = 50 Hz.`},{id:`e12`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`RMS of an ideal square wave equals...`,options:[`its peak value`,`peak/√2`,`half its peak`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`A square wave is at full amplitude for the whole cycle.`},{id:`e13`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A true-RMS meter differs from an average-sensing meter by...`,options:[`computing actual RMS regardless of waveform shape`,`always reading higher`,`only working on DC`,`requiring a trigger`],answer:0,explain:`Average-sensing meters assume a sine shape.`},{id:`e14`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The waveform with the lowest THD is...`,options:[`pure sine`,`square wave`,`modified sine`,`unfiltered PWM`],answer:0,explain:`A pure sine is essentially just its fundamental.`},{id:`e15`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A rising ESR in an electrolytic capacitor usually indicates...`,options:[`an ageing capacitor, often before capacitance drops much`,`a healthy capacitor`,`a wrong voltage rating`,`nothing significant`],answer:0,explain:`ESR often rises first as a capacitor ages.`},{id:`e16`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Near-zero ESR with no measurable capacitance indicates...`,options:[`a shorted capacitor`,`an excellent capacitor`,`an open capacitor`,`correct rating`],answer:0,explain:`Check ESR and capacitance together.`},{id:`e17`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A common guideline minimum for insulation resistance on a final circuit at 500 V DC is...`,options:[`1 MΩ`,`1 Ω`,`1 kΩ`,`10 Ω`],answer:0,explain:`A widely used general guideline.`},{id:`e18`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why is the insulation resistance tester simulation-only in this course?`,options:[`It intentionally applies a hazardous voltage to a circuit of unknown condition`,`It is not a real instrument`,`There is no safety concern`,`It only works on AC`],answer:0,explain:`That combination is exactly the hazard the simulation-only policy addresses.`},{id:`e19`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A shiny, low-emissivity surface on an infrared camera tends to...`,options:[`under-read the true temperature`,`over-read the true temperature`,`read perfectly`,`disable the camera`],answer:0,explain:`Low emissivity causes under-reading unless corrected.`},{id:`e20`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Completing every exercise in this laboratory means a student is...`,options:[`more competent at interpreting measurements, but not certified to work independently on live equipment`,`certified to work independently on live electrical equipment`,`exempt from further supervision`,`qualified as an electrician`],answer:0,explain:`Virtual exercises build judgement; they never substitute for supervised, qualified real-world training and certification.`}]}};export{t as F5};